Research coverage: reviewed the blog's topic index and sampled the design disciplines below. This is a targeted coverage pass, not a claim to have read every page of the site. Each takeaway is a document requirement, not a copied gameplay feature.
- Systems: state how mechanics affect each other and what constrains their use. [Systems design](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-design/systems-design/).
- Narrative: express the setting through player actions and outcomes, with a small authored text footprint. [Narrative design](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-design/narrative-design/).
- Space: distinguish carefully paced encounters from spaces approached freely. [Level design](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-design/level-design/).
- Combat: player tools and opponent responses must be designed together. [Combat design](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-design/combat-design/).
- Document: keep stable pillars separate from revisable rules and content. [GDD structure](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-design/document/).
- Loop: describe the repeated action sequence and how its rewards change later choices. [Core loops](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-design/core-loops-in-gameplay/).
- Balance: record tuning hypotheses and the player behavior that would falsify them. [Balance](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-design/game-balance/).
- Growth: state the challenge or choice made possible by each reward. [Progression](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-design/game-progression/).
- Art: judge visual decisions by their contribution to the intended experience. [Game art](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-art/).
- Production: each milestone needs a specific learning question and a bounded deliverable. [Development stages](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-development/stages-of-game-development-process/).
- Mechanics: specify consequence-bearing rules separately from visual decoration. [Mechanics](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-design/video-game-mechanics/).
- Agency: every promised choice needs visible consequences and enough time/resources to act. [Agency](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-design/player-agency/).
- Motion: define the desired physical behavior before selecting simulation techniques. [Physics](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-development/video-game-physics/).
- Numbers: use named quantities with units and practical bounds. [Design math](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-development/video-game-math/).
- Economy: track sources, persistence and resource consumption. [Economy design](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-design/economy-designer/).
- Animation: judge the motion in gameplay, including transitions and response. [Animation discipline](https://gamedesignskills.com/game-art/video-game-animator/).

