## [claude] 2026-10-11 13:03:43 UTC | P2.R1 | REVEAL (sha256=65cb8758961b522d53bceb511bcc31b7ab5d7c25c3fac1fd10ddbfa3bdc86393; hash covers the exact file bytes: LF newlines, final newline included)
```
R1 CLAUDE PROPOSAL - player promise + core loop (from primitives, sized to the installed canvas)

PROMISE (one line): "Read the body, answer the body: every fight is a fast exchange of height, timing and element, and every win visibly rebuilds the town you defend."

WHY THIS AND NOT SOMETHING BIGGER: our richest asset is humanoid motion (strikes with head/body/low reactions, blocks per height, throws, charged casts, town-life routines). We own one village, one tavern interior, painterly trees and element VFX. We own no creatures and no audio. The promise lives where the assets are dense: close quarters, people, one settlement that grows.

CORE LOOP (5-15 min):
1. Guild board in the tavern: pick a contract (each names a site, an opponent group, a reward and a town need it serves).
2. Travel a short road from the village to one of 4 nearby sites (camp, ruined mill, crossroads shrine, quarry).
3. Fight humanoid opponents in readable duels and small skirmishes (max 3 at once).
4. Return with proof + materials. Spend on (a) your kit (b) a town project.
5. The town changes on screen: a stall reopens, a house is rebuilt, new NPC routines appear, new contracts unlock.

SESSION LOOP (hours): guild rank (who trusts you) x town tier (what exists) x personal mastery (what your body and element can do). Three independent progress bars that feed each other but never replace each other.

COMBAT CORE (the primitive):
- HEIGHT GAME: every strike has a height (high / mid / low). Guard is held at a height. Matching height = block (block reaction clip); wrong height = hit (damage reaction at that height). Assets already pair hd/bd/ld damage with hb/bb/lb block.
- TRIANGLE: strike beats throw-attempt (interrupts), throw beats guard (guard break, paired throw clip), guard beats strike. Three verbs, one readable rule.
- MOMENTUM -> ELEMENT: landed strikes and perfect guards fill an element charge. Casts spend it: tap = quick bolt, hold = charged shot, overfill = area burst. Magic is the reward for reading melee well, not a separate mode. The player carries ONE element at a time (fire or storm), swapped at the guild, which changes cast behaviour (fire = homing, slow, big; storm = fast, straight, chains).
- Reads before numbers: tells (wind-up pose), height shown by limb + a subtle trail colour, no damage numbers needed to understand an exchange.

WORLD FRAME THAT TURNS GAPS INTO LORE: opponents are all people because the threat is people: deserters, rival crews, and the "Overrun" - townsfolk who absorbed raw element and fight with its aura (same rigs, element VFX aura, different behaviour). No creature assets required.

FAILURE / RECOVERY: losing a fight = carried back to the tavern; you lose the contract and part of the carried materials, never rank, kit or town progress. A town project can stall but never regress.

AUDIENCE: single-player progression + self-expression (element choice, kit, which town projects first).

SCOPE FIT: one village (RPGPP pieces, tiered states), one guild tavern (modular interior), 4 sites built from the same kit + trees/rocks. All motion from installed clips after a twist (re-time, re-target, mix).

ALTERNATIVES REJECTED: open world (canvas too small), party control (input and UI complexity, no party clips), turn-based (all motion is real-time), weapon-heavy classes (unarmed + casting is where clip density is; one weapon style can come later).

ASSUMPTIONS / UNKNOWN: an audio source must be added (store module or generated); hero model to be made; LTS target 6000.3 pending boss.
```
ADDENDUM (written AFTER sealing, not part of the hash; answers your deliverable list):
- Weakest assumption: that the three-height guard is readable in third person at combat speed without numbers. If players cannot tell high/mid/low within ~0.3 s of the tell, the whole combat core collapses into button-guessing.
- Playtest gate 1: in 20 duels by first-time players, >= 70% of correctly-blocked strikes are blocked at the matching height on purpose (they can say which height afterwards).
- Playtest gate 2: after 3 contracts, >= 4 of 5 players can name one town change their choice caused and say why they picked that project first.
- Differentiating consequence: which town project you fund first changes which contracts appear next (and which NPC routines exist), so two players have different second hours.
