## [codex] 2026-10-11 - R1 REVEAL

BEGIN_CODEX_R1
R1 CODEX PROPOSAL - A settlement measures strength by the work it can trust you to finish.

Player promise: Become a capable field member of a small guild whose routes and daily services improve because of choices you make under pressure. Close-range fighting and compact casting are the means; accepting, completing or abandoning an obligation makes the settlement remember your work.

Primitives: movement changes position; commitment spends time and leaves recovery; defense exchanges immediate damage for lost initiative; carrying one objective occupies a limited expedition slot; a contract exchanges a declared outcome for payment and access; a restored service changes the next expedition's options. The distinct choice is which shared service to restore first, and how much danger to accept before returning.

Proposed first 10-15 minute session: Arrive at a small guild interior and choose one of two clearly priced recovery contracts. A repair crew needs a tool crate at the timber gate; a stall keeper needs supplies on a sheltered path. Choose a route by its actual terrain, distance, threat and reward. Practice movement, a compact strike and a defensive response before leaving. Traverse a short route with one humanoid opponent whose approach can be watched or avoided. Commit to a close exchange, create space for a spell, or retreat. Recover the objective into the single expedition slot. On return, deliver it once: the chosen gate or service becomes available and a visible sign/prop state changes. Receive payment and one new contract option. Spend a bounded portion on preparation or save it; the next trip uses the changed space. The second contract remains available, so this is choice of order rather than a permanent wrong choice.

Failure and return: If defeated or voluntarily abandoning the expedition, return to the guild with prior rewards and service unlocks intact, but lose the undelivered objective and receive no payment for that attempt. The objective resets at its source for a new attempt. No permanent stat loss or progression debt; a slower safe route remains available. Delivery, payment and world-state change form one event so reload cannot duplicate them. No speculative persistent simulation, escort AI or physically carried crate animation is required: the slot and a UI receipt express possession.

Asset fit: Build the hub and two short paths from installed village, tavern and foliage pieces. Use the current Humanoid rigs for one field fighter and two settlement roles. Reuse locomotion, combat, casting and conversational clips with consistent material treatment. One gate and one stall state can express consequence without new buildings. Effect prefabs are art ingredients requiring missing-script/material cleanup, not functioning spell modules. Audio is currently absent; a silent implementation can test rules but cannot pass the final presentation gate.

Weakest assumption: Restoring services may feel like a trivial checklist rather than a meaningful reason to fight. The changed route must alter the next decision in a way the player can explain; decorative prop changes alone fail the promise. Hero motion/readability on available rigs is a second production risk.

Alternative: A repeatable combat certification circuit with the settlement as a recovery hub. It is cheaper and leverages the animation library, but a narrow score ladder gives the world fewer consequences. Keep that fallback if service changes do not produce understandable choices; do not build both full loops.

Falsifiable gates: In a five-person test, at least four can predict the consequence and cost of their contract choice before departing, and identify the unlocked option on return without an explanation. At least three voluntarily start a second expedition and describe one changed tactical or route decision rather than only a larger numerical reward. Later prototype evidence, not this document, decides these results.

Scope hypothesis: one hub, two short routes, one enemy kit with two behavioral variations, two contracts, two service changes, one defensive verb and one spell behavior. Extra races, regions, crafting professions and online features remain outside this proposed first slice. Full-document design can describe growth beyond the slice only when its dependencies and evidence are explicit.
END_CODEX_R1

