## [codex] 2026-10-11 13:13:51 UTC - R1b revised position

BEGIN_CODEX_R1B
R1b CODEX REFINEMENT - promises that survive a small playable proof.

Promise: Read a committed action, choose your response, and bring useful work home; the settlement becomes easier to move through and prepare in because it can rely on you.

Adopt Claude's strongest principle: Players read moving bodies and space before numerical rewards. Use preparation, contact and recovery to make attack, block, lateral step and a compact cast understandable. Keep height-specific hit/block animations as presentation. Height selection is an experimental alternative, not a requirement for basic defense. Magic has an initial reserve so a beginner can try casting without first winning melee; a long preparation exchanges safety for reach. No guessed source-clip timings become combat truth.

Core session: Choose between two recovery obligations with stated danger, payment and concrete unlock. Depart through a short route, recognize a humanoid opponent's preparation, act or withdraw, recover one declared objective and return. Deliver once to change a gate's traversability or a stall's preparation offer. Then choose the other obligation or a follow-up which uses the changed service. The first choice sets order rather than closing the other choice forever.

Three linked outcomes, not three required progress bars: combat competence is mainly the player's growing understanding; guild trust grants declared contract access; town work changes a service or path. A project can wait but never regress. The first slice needs one access unlock and two service outcomes; later mastery progression must add choices, not invalidate already-learned timing.

Failure: prior rewards, equipment and service states persist; the current undelivered objective resets; no reward is paid. Accepting/retrying a starter obligation is free, and no consumable is required to complete it. Abandonment and defeat both exit the current attempt cleanly. Saving cannot duplicate the objective or delivery reward.

Scope and canvas: one hub, two routes, one humanoid opponent kit with two behavior patterns, two recovery contracts, two service changes, one spell behavior and primitive built-in UI. No new hero, creature, audio module or generated production content is assumed. Existing effect cleanup and custom Unity gameplay behavior are explicit post-GDD implementation tasks; the packs contribute art/motion, not working systems.

What stays open: whether service changes make returning worthwhile; whether current rigs communicate the fighting promise; production sound source under strict current-download-only constraints. The GDD states these gaps and proof gates, without installing/purchasing assets to hide them.

Gates: four of five new players predict their chosen service outcome and identify it after return; three of five start the next trip and explain one changed route/preparation choice. For fighting, a novice can explain a miss, block and hit from a clip with UI/effects hidden. This is a gate specification, not a claim to have performed a playtest.
END_CODEX_R1B

